-## Local source with the CLI
+## Source and deployment
 
-Use this path when you can run shell commands and `pluginEngine apps --help` works.
-If the command is missing, or it has no `framework` subcommand, install the
-current release. An older CLI can also fail against a newer host with `NotFound`
-or `UnexpectedResponse`. Install it with npm, or without Node on macOS or Linux
-with the install script:
+Keep an app's source as a complete list of `{ path, content }` files, including
+`index.ts` and `package.json`. Pin the exact `apps` version returned by the
+host's framework release operation. For example:
 
-```sh
-npm i -g pluginEngine@beta --include=optional
-curl -fsSL https://pluginEngine.sh/docs/install.sh | sh
-```
-
-The `beta` tag is required. The `latest` tag installs the previous PluginEngine,
-which has no `apps` command. Installing globally changes the user's machine,
-so ask first if that has not been approved.
-
-Keep one directory per app with `index.ts` and `package.json` at its root. The
-CLI sends the whole directory, skipping `.git`, `node_modules` and `.DS_Store`.
-Keep scratch files out of it; files missing from the directory are deleted
-from the app. `--files` also accepts a JSON file holding an array of
-`{ "path", "content" }` objects.
-
-Code, every file outside `ui/public/`, is UTF-8 text: at most 4,096 files and
-16 MiB of file contents, on every host. Each of the app's own JavaScript or
-TypeScript files is at most 2,000,000 bytes (2 MB). JSON files are outside this
-limit, so keep a large document such as an OpenAPI description in a `.json`
-file. The host checks this limit while it builds, not the CLI before uploading:
-a build that reaches a larger file of the app's own fails with
-`VersionBuildFailed` at the `source` stage, naming the file, its size and the
-limit; no app or version is created. Files inside npm packages the app imports
-are not limited and are still compiled. On self-host and the local server a
-build compiles on the thread that runs apps, so a large package file delays
-every app call, and any other request served from that thread, while it
-compiles: a 3,000,000-byte module of dense code took about 1.2 seconds in our
-tests. Files under `ui/public/` are served as
-they are and may be binary, such as images, fonts, audio, WebAssembly or a
-prebuilt bundle: at most 10,000 files, each at most 32 MB and 1 GB in total.
-Builds never load them; each deploy copies them to the host's own storage.
-The CLI checks the other limits before it uploads anything. A source over them
-fails with `SourceRejected`, which states the count or size and names the
-largest files; a binary file outside `ui/public/` fails the same way and is
-named. The host rejects a larger source with `SourceError` and HTTP 413.
-
-A directory of at most 16 MiB in total is sent as files, with binary files as
-base64. A larger one is pushed with Git: `pluginEngine apps create`, `commit` and
-`deploy --files` do this themselves, send only the files the app does not
-already hold, and split large uploads into several pushes. Deploy a directory
-in one step with `pluginEngine apps deploy --app <app-id> --files ./app`.
-
-Reading source lists a `ui/public/` file that is not UTF-8 text, or does not
-fit beside the rest of the source, as `{ "path", "object", "size" }` without
-its bytes. Send that entry back unchanged in a commit to keep the file. Through
-`appManagement.commit`, send a small binary file as
-`{ "path", "content": "<base64>", "encoding": "base64" }`. `pluginEngine apps source
---app <app-id> --out ./copy` writes every file, binary files included, and a
-Git clone has them all.
-
-Read these docs with
-`pluginEngine skills --app pluginEngine --name app-authoring --file deploy.md`.
-
-Commands use `--host`, then `PLUGIN_ENGINE_HOST`, then the host the CLI signed in to
-last. Without any of them, they use the local background PluginEngine, starting it
-if it is not running, and need no API key. A set
-`PLUGIN_ENGINE_API_KEY` is sent in place of the background PluginEngine's own
-credential, so it must be that server's current key. A `--host` that names
-another local server, such as `http://127.0.0.1:4400`, needs that server's API
-key in `PLUGIN_ENGINE_API_KEY`; if the variable is unset, ask the user to set it, and
-do not search files for it. For hosted PluginEngine, run
-`pluginEngine apps login` once; add `--host <origin>` for a self-hosted PluginEngine.
-It prints a link and a code and waits; ask the user to open the link and
-approve the code. Later commands use that host without `--host`.
-`--organization` defaults to the organization you signed in to.
-
-A command that fails exits with status 1. Its standard error states the
-message, the error code, the HTTP status when the host answered, and the
-host's recovery: `Next step` for the user and `For agents` for you. When
-standard output is not a terminal, the command also prints the same failure
-there as JSON, so scripts can read it:
-
-```json
-{
-  "error": {
-    "code": "VersionBuildFailed",
-    "status": 422,
-    "message": "App build failed at the declaration stage: ...",
-    "details": {
-      "stage": "declaration",
-      "location": { "file": "index.ts", "line": 3, "column": 7 }
-    }
-  }
-}
-```
-
-`recovery` holds `action` and `instructions` when the error declares them;
-follow the instructions. `details` holds the error's other fields. When a newer
-CLI release exists, `update` says so with the install command, and standard error
-ends with the same line; update the CLI before retrying.
