-## Updating a hosted app
-
-Use the shared commit and deploy workflow to edit an existing app. `appManagement.source`
-reads working Git source; `apps.source` reads immutable deployed source. Saving
-one does not change the other. Read both when you need to compare pending edits
-with the running app.
-
-```js
-const pluginEngine = tools.pluginEngine;
-const path = { organization: "<approved-organization-id>", app: "<app-id>" };
-const source = await pluginEngine.appManagement.source({ path });
-const entry = source.files.find((file) => file.path === "index.ts");
-if (!entry || entry.content.split("<exact old text>").length !== 2) {
-  throw new Error("Expected one match in index.ts; review the edit.");
-}
-const files = source.files.map((file) =>
-  file.path === entry.path
-    ? { ...file, content: file.content.replace("<exact old text>", "<replacement text>") }
-    : file,
-);
-const saved = await pluginEngine.appManagement.commit({
-  path,
-  body: { expected: source.revision.commit, files, message: "Update app" },
-});
-if (saved.removed.count > 0) {
-  throw new Error(
-    `The save removed ${saved.removed.count} files: ${saved.removed.paths.join(", ")}`,
-  );
-}
-return await pluginEngine.appManagement.deploy({
-  path,
-  body: { commit: saved.revision.commit },
-});
-```
-
-Commit sends the complete file list; omitted files are deleted. The result's
-`removed` has their `count` and at most 100 of their `paths`; check it before
-deploying, as above. A deleted file is still in `source.files`: add it back and
-commit against `saved.revision.commit`. A stale
-`expected` returns `SourceError` with `reason: "conflict"`. Read working source
-again and reconcile the edits before retrying. Deploying returns `{ app,
-version }`, preserves the app ID and data, and never updates the Git branch.
-It has no expected-active-version argument. Check the app's linked accounts
-after changing account requirements; an account whose provider no requirement
-uses no longer applies.
-
-Every call follows the active version, so nothing needs updating to pick up
-new code. The next `execute` lists and calls the new tools. Activating a
-version re-registers each webhook source's subscriptions on it in the
-background, usually within seconds, without blocking tool calls; the app's
-Webhooks tab shows their status. A manual (`setup`) subscription keeps its
-callback URL, secret and setup fields while the activated version keeps the same
-`signingSecret` and accepts the same `state` fields; changing either needs a new setup. It also deletes the saved settings of schedules
-the new version no longer declares.
-
-`apps.versions` lists retained versions. `apps.source` accepts an optional
-`query.version` to read a specific version. `apps.activate` requires
-`body: { version, expectedVersion }`, where `expectedVersion` is the
-app's current active version. A stale value returns `AppVersionChanged`.
-Activation only changes which code runs; it does not reverse app data or changes
-made in external services. Source reads and deploys follow the
-hosted product’s access rules. Discover tools again in a new execute after changing code.
-
-`apps.rename` changes the app's name and the slug derived from it; the app ID,
-accounts, links and data stay. A new slug changes the agent namespace
-`tools.<slug>`, the Git clone URL and, on hosted, the page address
-`appUi.location` returns, so old addresses stop working. Local page addresses
-use the app ID and do not change. No operation changes the slug alone.
+For locally authored files, construct the `{ path, content }` list with JSON
+serialization. Insert that serialized value as JavaScript data in the codemode
+payload. Do not interpolate arbitrary source into shell commands or manually
+constructed template literals. Submit file contents, since remote execution
+cannot read a local path.
 
